---
import Layout from "@/layouts/ProjectDetailLayout.astro";
import HoverScrambleText from "@/components/animation/hover-scramble-text.astro";
import PRBContainer from "@/components/ui/prb-container.astro";
import PRBText from "@/components/ui/prb-text.astro";
import PRBImage from "@/components/ui/prb-image.astro";
import Chip from "@/components/ui/chip.astro";
import PrbOrList from "@/components/ui/prb-or-list.astro";

import projectOverviewImage from "@/assets/images/project-1.jpg";
---

<Layout title="Ree - Project PT Sari Alam">
    <main class="bg-[#FAFAFA]">
        <div class="w-full max-w-[1680px] py-21.5 xl:py-25 px-4 md:px-6">
            <div class="flex flex-col gap-16 xl:gap-4 xl:grid xl:grid-cols-16">
                <aside id="sidebar" class="xl:col-span-5">
                    <a href="/" class="font-medium font-pixels tracking-tight text-gray-400 hover:text-gray-600 transition-colors">
                        Back
                    </a>
                </aside>

                <section class="xl:col-span-9 xl:col-start-8 space-y-6">
                    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl xl:text-5xl leading-9 md:leading-11 xl:leading-14 tracking-tight font-medium text-blue-950">
                        From Shift Bottlenecks to Split-Second Scans: Engineering an AI Attendance Ecosystem for PT Sari Alam Sukabumi
                    </h1>

                    <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2">
                        <Chip>React.Js</Chip>
                        <Chip>Go</Chip>
                        <Chip>Computer Vision</Chip>
                        <Chip>Tailwind CSS</Chip>
                        <Chip>TypeScript</Chip>
                    </div>

                    <PRBContainer>
                        <PRBImage src={projectOverviewImage} />
                        <PRBText>
                            At PT Sari Alam Sukabumi, manufacturing operations move fast, but morning shift turnstiles used to move remarkably slow. With hundreds of factory operators and back-office
                            staff checking in each day, physical badge readers and manual logs routinely created long entry queues. Worse, physical cards left the door open to proxy clock-ins ("buddy
                            punching"), while HR teams spent hours manually reconciling disjointed paper sheets against shift schedules.
                        </PRBText>

                        <PRBText>
                            Behind the scenes, administrative workflows were just as stalled. Shift managers were chasing down paper leave requests, while HR personnel spent hours manually reconciling
                            physical records against shift rosters for payroll.
                        </PRBText>

                        <PRBText>
                            The company needed a connected, tamper-proof operational backbone. Stepping in as UI/UX Designer and Frontend Developer, I set out to architect and build a unified
                            three-app ecosystem:
                        </PRBText>

                        <PrbOrList
                            data={[
                                "The Frontliner Gate Terminal: A dedicated, kiosk-style web application mounted at entrance checkpoint computers, combining physical RFID badge tapping with biometric face verification.",
                                "The HR Employee Management System: A full-featured administration platform for HR to manage comprehensive employee records, monitor company-wide attendance patterns,",
                                "The HR Employee Management System: A full-featured administration platform for HR to manage comprehensive employee records, monitor company-wide attendance patterns, and oversee overall leave and overtime compliance.",
                                "The Manager Portal: A streamlined management dashboard tailored specifically for shift supervisors and department leads to review, approve, or reject team leave requests and overtime submissions in real time.",
                            ]}
                        />

                        <PRBText>
                            Before writing any code, I mapped the divergent user journeys in Figma. A factory worker standing at a physical gate in high-traffic conditions requires an entirely
                            different interaction model than a manager approving an overtime request on a laptop, or an HR specialist auditing employee records across multiple shifts.
                        </PRBText>

                        <PRBText>
                            For the Frontliner system at the entrance gate, the user interface had to get out of the way. When a worker taps their card on the connected RFID reader, the screen
                            instantly pulls up their profile, activates the camera viewport, and triggers the face verification pipeline within milliseconds. Every button, status indicator, and prompt
                            was designed with high visual contrast and bold feedback so workers could confirm their status at a glance without breaking stride.
                        </PRBText>

                        <PRBText>
                            When architecting the frontend, the core challenge was handling real-time camera streams, canvas processing, and high-frequency UI updates without dropping frames.
                        </PRBText>

                        <PRBText>
                            For the Management Systems, the priorities shifted to workflow efficiency and role-specific clarity. The Manager Portal was kept lean and action-oriented—designed so
                            supervisors could review shift context, evaluate leave or overtime requests, and process approvals with minimal clicks. Meanwhile, the HR Platform was built for
                            high-density oversight, equipped with advanced filtering, employee profile management, and comprehensive audit logs that HR teams could work within all day without visual
                            fatigue.
                        </PRBText>

                        <PRBText>
                            The core frontend challenge lay in orchestrating peripheral hardware inputs, live camera video streams, and responsive dashboard updates into a smooth, unified user
                            experience.
                        </PRBText>

                        <PRBText>
                            A common instinct for biometric setups is streaming raw video frames to a remote server for processing. In a manufacturing setting, running continuous video streams over
                            local plant networks creates latency and risks gate slowdowns during network hiccups. To eliminate this bottleneck, we deployed client-side computer vision models running
                            directly inside the browser using WebAssembly and WebGL canvas acceleration. The browser directly managed the camera feed, isolated the facial vector mesh, and verified
                            identity locally against biometric signatures the moment the RFID card identified the employee.
                        </PRBText>

                        <PRBText>
                            To structure this architecture, React.js was the core library of choice. React's state management made handling the hardware lifecycle—detecting USB/serial RFID reader
                            inputs, mounting the camera stream, and transitioning through verification states—predictable and maintainable. It also allowed us to establish shared component foundations
                            across all three applications.
                        </PRBText>

                        <PRBText>
                            For styling and interface components, Tailwind CSS paired with ShadCN UI gave us granular design control without the bloated bundle sizes and rigid style overrides typical
                            of heavy UI frameworks. To keep the back-office portals synchronized with the entrance gates, TanStack Query handled server-state caching and background re-fetching,
                            ensuring that HR and managers saw incoming attendance data and approval requests as they happened.
                        </PRBText>

                        <PRBText>Deploying an automated physical checkpoint combined with multi-tier approval workflows surfaced real-world edge cases.</PRBText>

                        <PRBText>
                            The most critical issue was securing the entrance gate against spoofing and proxy check-ins. Simply matching a face to an RFID card was vulnerable to bad actors holding up
                            a printed photo or a smartphone image of a colleague to the lens. To counter this, I implemented an active liveness detection pipeline alongside the face match. Once the
                            worker taps their RFID card, the system prompts for a dynamic, randomized micro-action—such as an eye blink cadence or a subtle head turn within a three-second window. Only
                            when authentic physical depth and motion were verified alongside facial vectors would the frontliner kiosk log the timestamp and emit the green confirmation signal.
                        </PRBText>

                        <PRBText>
                            A secondary challenge was hardware resilience. The Frontliner gate computer ran continuously throughout day and night shifts. Running continuous computer vision inference
                            on an active camera feed in an open browser tab quickly led to memory leaks and processor thermal throttling, causing frame drops and input lag.
                        </PRBText>

                        <PRBText>
                            To solve this, I decoupled the video stream from active inference. The camera feed remains idle in a standby loop until an event listener captures the RFID card swipe. Only
                            then does the facial detection model spin up, execute the liveness and match sequence, submit the encrypted payload, and immediately release its compute resources back to
                            the browser. This eliminated memory bloat and ensured the terminal remained responsive all day.
                        </PRBText>

                        <PRBText>
                            Finally, managing the back-office experience meant handling multi-tier authorization cleanly. Because HR administrators and department managers utilized separate management
                            platforms with overlapping data, we established distinct permission layers and reusable data-grid components. By leveraging virtualized list rendering with TanStack Virtual
                            in the HR portal, tables displaying hundreds of employee shift records could be sorted, filtered, and audited at 60 FPS without DOM lag.
                        </PRBText>

                        <PRBText>
                            By uniting physical entry points with digital approval pipelines, the new ecosystem transformed daily operations at PT Sari Alam Sukabumi. The combined
                            RFID-and-face-verification terminal eliminated proxy attendance while keeping entry lines moving rapidly during shift changes. Meanwhile, supervisors and HR teams replaced
                            paper request forms and manual spreadsheets with dedicated portals for instant approvals and automated record-keeping. The project proved that client-side web technologies
                            can seamlessly bridge physical gate hardware and enterprise workflows in demanding operational environments.
                        </PRBText>
                    </PRBContainer>
                </section>
            </div>
        </div>
    </main>

    <HoverScrambleText />
</Layout>

